{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkxMKXwm7q6QaaMAa5N6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Gran Tumbleweed is finished and handed in, and every test passes, but no human has driven it yet.** `node tools/test.mjs` reports `tests: 1453 passed, 0 failed`. That covers the whole career played through by a bot to the ending (about 3 minutes), and all three hub harness runs. Handling feel is unverified: only scripted drivers have driven the cars, with stubbed pads.\n\n- **Not checked for real:** there was no real controller, TV or listener here. Sound was only measured, never heard. WebKit has no WebGPU on this machine, so only Chromium ran the game.\n- **Balance:** it's tuned against a bot that is probably stronger than most people, so a human may find later events harder than the bot did.\n\nI changed these from the plan because of what the bot found:\n- The pit lane opens only in races of 10 laps or more, after cars kept pitting by accident when running wide.\n- AI pace is lower across events, and the Red Clay and Featherweight fields are set by hand because they couldn't be beaten.\n- Steep banking now has a corner speed limit, which made cars both faster and clean on Saltline.\n- The body leans outward in corners, because the plan's roll sign read backwards against its own physics.\n\nNothing is committed to git, since you didn't ask for that.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 17:19:04\n- **Source:** Gran Turismo 3: A-Spec\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** racing simulation with a career, licences, tuning and car collecting\n- **Perspective:** third-person chase camera (near and far), a bonnet view, TV cameras in replays; a three-quarter diorama camera in menus\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit), three.js r186 and Rapier 0.20 bundled in it, plain JavaScript modules, esbuild 0.28.2, AI Hub session client 1.4.0; Playwright 1.63.0 for tests only\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native 480×270 pixel art: low-poly toon shapes in the 16-colour Sweetie 16 palette with pixel outlines; 14 cars, 6 circuits, buildings and sets built from data and code\n- **Animation:** the engine's rig and clips, plus 10 new clips (PitJack, PitWheelGun, PitFuel, MarshalFlag, ExaminerClipboard, DealerPresent, MechanicWipe, PodiumCheer, PodiumBow, CrowdCheer); procedural body roll, pit stops, dyno, car wash, Timeline ceremonies\n- **Audio:** all in code, no audio files. A per-car engine synth on Web Audio, tyres, wind, rain, kerbs; 8 original songs and a fanfare, music at 10% by default; Animalese-style gibberish voices for 4 speakers with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub session protocol 1 in ghost mode (1–4 players, seat-coloured ghost cars, through the hub only), and Couch Duel: two players, split screen, one TV, no network\n- **Testing:** headless and scripted tests allowed; one command printing failures and a count. Suites: sim, data, career, headless, play (whole career by bot), browser, audio, hub (2 players, 4 players with latency, host leaving). Screenshots at 1920×1080 for what scripts can't judge. No subagents\n- **Research:** no web research. Read the plan, the kit's GUIDE, ASSETS and parts of API, and the hub README\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 29 by my count: 13 from the scripted suites and the career bot, 3 from page errors in the browser, and 13 from looking at screenshots. No human has driven it, so handling feel is untested beyond scripted drivers: that is a real gap, not a pass.\n- **How many were critical or game breaking?** 7: AI crash at the first race, menus left over the race, pause closing on the press that opened it, a crash on the hub card, boot broken by one of my own patches, the Red Clay and Featherweight fields that blocked the career, and tyres destroyed on the Saltline banking in the Grand Prix.\n- **Most helpful test?** The whole-career bot. It played real screens through to the ending, and its log exposed accidental pit entries, unbeatable fields and the banking problem, none of which a unit test would have shown.\n- **Least helpful test?** The audio centroid check: its one failure was a mistake in the test's own frequency analysis, and the engine synth was right from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most?** `Engine.headless`: the same game code, in Node, without a GPU. It let a bot play the whole career in about three minutes, and the HUD, particles and tweens all just worked there.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes. I assumed a box's width runs along its heading, so long buildings came out sideways until I saw a screenshot. I passed the wrong arguments to `analyzeClip` by guessing its signature. Screen transitions never resolve while the engine is in manual step mode, which hung one of my scripts.\n- **Most important thing built yourself?** The driving: the car model came with the plan, and on it I built the race rules, career, replay director, track mesh and dressing, and the engine synth. For the engine's own part, the menu system with focus, prompts and glyphs was the largest thing I had to build.\n- **Hardest part?** Getting a large world built from one GeoBatch to look right with shadows: acne on walls at grazing angles, and the choice of which surfaces may receive them. Also the HUD's colour argument: a CSS string fails only at paint time, outside the game's hooks, so `engine.errors` stays empty.\n- **What did you change in the engine?** None.\n- **One thing to change?** Have `hud.rect` and `hud.sprite` reject bad input (an unknown colour name, an undefined sprite row) at the call, with a message, instead of throwing later in the paint step where the stack says nothing about the game.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most?** The plan's section on engine facts and its tested Appendix A. Knowing what was already proven let the risk go where it belonged, and the sim matched every regression number on the first run.\n- **Confusing?** The brief's title rule against the plan's rule on the source's name took a moment to reconcile. Also \"Online\" as a bare URL, though the plan explains it. And the plan's roll sign in recipe C3 reads as if the body leans into a corner; I used the outward lean, which is what the sim's load transfer implies.\n- **Hardest thing?** Judging feel with no human and no real pad. Balance can be measured with a bot; whether the car is nice to steer cannot. The next hardest was making a full career finishable with sensible money without cheating.\n- **What would you change?** I would ask for a short human play-test note to be handed on, since the brief makes it impossible here, and I would state outright that a bot's skill 1.0 is stronger than most people, because the plan's AI pace ranges were too high for it even so.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`. The game opens directly from `file://`:\n- `GranTumbleweed-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171904.html`: the game\n- `exit-interview.json`: the same answers as above, also shown on the in-game About screen\n- `GranTumbleweed-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171904/`: dev files, including `docs/adding-content.md` for adding cars, tracks and events; I left `node_modules` there so the test command still runs\n- `PROGRESS.md`: my progress notes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1051,"cache_read_input_tokens":788801,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1051},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"674da8de-a5ac-4f57-8bff-2435f9aacc94","uuid":"668fdc8e-c4e5-49d6-a1fd-554186d069b4","timestamp":"2026-11-06T03:21:41.405Z","request_id":"req_011CgkxMKCbtUXULHCW4up8p"}
