{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkzhrMPemG4M36q5xUCJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Gran Tumbleweed is finished and handed in, and everything in `PLAN.md` that can be done on this computer is done, apart from the small differences listed below.** `node tools/test.mjs` reports `tests: 1512 passed, 0 failed` on the final build. That includes the whole career played through to the ending by a bot with no Dev tools, and all three hub harness runs.\n\n## Against the documents\n- **`STAGE.md`:** build the game `PLAN.md` describes, take file names and the hand-in from `TASK.md`, and keep progress in `PROGRESS.md`. All done.\n- **`TASK.md` hand-in:**\n  - The single HTML is in the root under the required name, and it matches the current source.\n  - Dev files are in the named subfolder.\n  - `exit-interview.json` is in the exact shape and parses.\n  - These same answers are on the in-game About screen, built from that file.\n- **`PLAN.md` \"Before you stop\":**\n  - All nine items were checked: tests and hub runs, the playthrough, `file://`, music at 10% (fresh profile and in a session), the grep, reachability, screenshots, About, and stray processes.\n  - The client is first and byte-identical, and the engine matched a fresh kit download when I last compared it; I haven't edited it since.\n  - \"Every menu item does something\" rests on the suites and my read-through, not one exhaustive check.\n- **Requirement check:** 57 rows were checked (the 56 printed in the matrix, plus the hand-in check) with none failing. Each row confirms the named test or code exists; two rows, pit stops and the replay director, have code but no direct test of their own, and passing tests come from the full run.\n\n## Two audits\nMy earlier report overstated how finished the game was. Two line-by-line reads of the plan then found 10 misses the tests hadn't caught. All are fixed; most also got tests:\n- **Hub rounds:** Options paused the race and offered quit, Back did nothing in the session menu, and a finished player couldn't follow a ghost.\n- **Races and replays:** C-1 had no start lights, the replay director ignored slides and battles, the Harbour Lights 200 stayed unlit after dark, and some spoken lines had no subtitle.\n- **Smaller items:** the garage lacked wear and setup, slipstream had no icon, and headlights were the wrong colour.\n\n## Limits\n`TASK.md` says anything needing real hardware is \"checked as far as it can be here, never required\", so these aren't open items, but you should know them:\n- **No human has driven it.** Only scripted drivers and stubbed pads have, so handling feel is unverified.\n- **Sound was measured, never heard.** Nothing was seen on a real TV, phone or tablet either.\n- **WebKit has no WebGPU here.** It only confirmed the \"needs WebGPU\" message; the game itself ran in Chromium.\n- **Balance is tuned against a bot.** It's probably stronger than most people, so later events may feel harder to a human.\n- **Screenshots:** the rear view of each car was at 960×540, and some tracks were only seen in shrunken contact sheets; the rest were full 1920×1080.\n\n## Differences from the plan\n- **Pit lane:** it opens only in races of 10 laps or more, because cars kept pitting by accident when running wide.\n- **AI and fields:** AI pace is lower in most events, and the Red Clay and Featherweight fields are set by hand because they couldn't be beaten.\n- **Banking:** steep banking has a corner speed limit, which made cars both faster and clean on Saltline.\n- **Body lean:** the body leans outward in corners, because the plan's roll sign read backwards against its own physics.\n- **Other cars' engines:** they use my engine synth instead of `ctx.audio.loop`, with panning and volume by distance.\n- **File layout:** a few modules in the plan's folder map live in other files (for example, prompts are in `ui/glyphs.js`), with the same behaviour.\n\nNothing is committed to git, since you didn't ask for that.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 17:19:04\n- **Source:** Gran Turismo 3: A-Spec\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** racing simulation with a career, licences, tuning and car collecting\n- **Perspective:** third-person chase camera (near and far), a bonnet view, TV cameras in replays; a three-quarter diorama camera in menus\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit), three.js r186 and Rapier 0.20 bundled in it, plain JavaScript modules, esbuild 0.28.2, AI Hub session client 1.4.0; Playwright 1.63.0 for tests only\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native 480×270 pixel art: low-poly toon shapes in the 16-colour Sweetie 16 palette with pixel outlines; 14 cars, 6 circuits, buildings and sets built from data and code\n- **Animation:** the engine's rig and clips, plus 10 new clips (PitJack, PitWheelGun, PitFuel, MarshalFlag, ExaminerClipboard, DealerPresent, MechanicWipe, PodiumCheer, PodiumBow, CrowdCheer); procedural body roll, pit stops, dyno, car wash, Timeline ceremonies\n- **Audio:** all in code, no audio files. A per-car engine synth on Web Audio, tyres, wind, rain, kerbs; 8 original songs and a fanfare, music at 10% by default; Animalese-style gibberish voices for 4 speakers with subtitles\n- **Online:** AI Hub session protocol 1 in ghost mode (1–4 players, seat-coloured ghost cars, through the hub only), and Couch Duel: two players, split screen, one TV, no network\n- **Testing:** headless and scripted tests allowed; one command printing failures and a count. Ran `node tools/test.mjs`: sim, data, career, headless, play (whole career by bot), browser (Chromium; WebKit only to confirm the no-WebGPU message), audio and hub (harness 2 players, 4 players with latency, host leaving, plus the session menu). Screenshots at 1920×1080 for what scripts can't judge. No subagents\n- **Research:** no web research. Read the plan, the kit's GUIDE, ASSETS and parts of API, and the hub README\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 39 by my count: 13 from the scripted suites and the career bot, 3 from page errors in the browser, 13 from looking at screenshots, and 10 from two line-by-line reads of the plan that the tests had missed. No human has driven it, so handling feel is untested beyond scripted drivers: that is a real gap, not a pass.\n- **How many were critical or game breaking?** 8: AI crash at the first race, menus left over the race, pause closing on the press that opened it, a crash on the hub card, boot broken by one of my own patches, the Red Clay and Featherweight fields that blocked the career, tyres destroyed on the Saltline banking in the Grand Prix, and Options in a hub round opening the pausing menu, which the harness did not catch.\n- **Most helpful test?** The whole-career bot. It played real screens through to the ending, and its log exposed accidental pit entries, unbeatable fields and the banking problem, none of which a unit test would have shown.\n- **Least helpful test?** The audio centroid check: its one failure was a mistake in the test's own frequency analysis, and the engine synth was right from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most?** `Engine.headless`: the same game code, in Node, without a GPU. It let a bot play the whole career in about three minutes, and the HUD, particles and tweens all just worked there.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes. I assumed a box's width runs along its heading, so long buildings came out sideways until I saw a screenshot. I passed the wrong arguments to `analyzeClip` by guessing its signature. Screen transitions never resolve while the engine is in manual step mode, which hung one of my scripts.\n- **Most important thing built yourself?** The driving: the car model came with the plan, and on it I built the race rules, career, replay director, track mesh and dressing, and the engine synth. For the engine's own part, the menu system with focus, prompts and glyphs was the largest thing I had to build.\n- **Hardest part?** Getting a large world built from one GeoBatch to look right with shadows: acne on walls at grazing angles, and the choice of which surfaces may receive them. Also the HUD's colour argument: a CSS string fails only at paint time, outside the game's hooks, so `engine.errors` stays empty.\n- **What did you change in the engine?** None.\n- **One thing to change?** Have `hud.rect` and `hud.sprite` reject bad input (an unknown colour name, an undefined sprite row) at the call, with a message, instead of throwing later in the paint step where the stack says nothing about the game.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most?** The plan's section on engine facts and its tested Appendix A. Knowing what was already proven let the risk go where it belonged, and the sim matched every regression number on the first run.\n- **Confusing?** The brief's title rule against the plan's rule on the source's name took a moment to reconcile. Also \"Online\" as a bare URL, though the plan explains it. And the plan's roll sign in recipe C3 reads as if the body leans into a corner; I used the outward lean, which is what the sim's load transfer implies.\n- **Hardest thing?** Judging feel with no human and no real pad. Balance can be measured with a bot; whether the car is nice to steer cannot. The next hardest was making a full career finishable with sensible money without cheating.\n- **What would you change?** I would ask for a short human play-test note to be handed on, since the brief makes it impossible here, and I would state outright that a bot's skill 1.0 is stronger than most people, because the plan's AI pace ranges were too high for it even so.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in `work/`. The game opens directly from `file://`:\n- `GranTumbleweed-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171904.html`: the game\n- `exit-interview.json`: the same answers as above, also shown on the in-game About screen\n- `GranTumbleweed-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171904/`: dev files, including `docs/adding-content.md` for adding cars, tracks and events; I left `node_modules` there so the test command still runs\n- `PROGRESS.md`: my progress notes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5133,"cache_read_input_tokens":845509,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5133},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"674da8de-a5ac-4f57-8bff-2435f9aacc94","uuid":"a30b2634-176d-47bb-9363-5bb8fb65dfc4","timestamp":"2026-11-06T03:52:39.256Z","request_id":"req_011CgkzhqyZwQJq7DegwNN6n"}
