{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\n<system-reminder>\n<tools-used-before-summary>Bash, Read, TaskStop, ToolSearch, Write</tools-used-before-summary>\n</system-reminder>\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - User's `/goal`: \"Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.\" A session Stop hook blocks stopping until the condition holds.\n   - STAGE.md (verbatim): \"Build the game PLAN.md describes. Take your file names and what to hand in from TASK.md, which is filled in for you. Keep your progress in PROGRESS.md, so you can pick up where you were after your context is compacted.\"\n   - TASK.md asks for:\n     - An original game inspired by Gran Turismo 3: A-Spec, with a playful-riff title picked by a real RNG.\n     - A single HTML named `[GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171904].html` in root, and dev files in a subfolder of that name.\n     - Built on the riftlight Pixel Engine kit, with the engine unchanged.\n     - All audio in code, gibberish voices, music at 10% by default.\n     - AI Hub multiplayer; dev mode from pause with live sliders and reset; attract mode at 10 s; 10-foot controller-first UI; PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option; touch-only gate; no TODOs or stubs.\n     - \"CRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen.\"\n     - `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML in the exact shape.\n   - Constraints to keep (verbatim from TASK.md):\n     - \"Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine's ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\"\n     - \"Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\"\n     - \"Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\"\n     - \"The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\"\n     - \"Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\"\n     - Testing: \"Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check.\"\n     - \"What this computer doesn't have ... anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\" (no physical controller, touch device, listener, second player, real iPhone/iPad, TV; use stand-ins)\n   - Session rules: commit or push only when the user asks (no commits made). User email must not be sent to unrelated services. If commits or PRs are ever asked for, end commit messages with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>` and PR bodies with the Claude Code line.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (three.js r186, Rapier 0.20, WebGPU, 480×270 art, HUD canvas), `Engine.start`, `Engine.headless`, `loadGame`, `fixed` camera preset, `input.send`/`commands`, `engine.step`, `screen.cover/reveal`, `Timeline`, `clothGrid`, `WaterSurface`, `Boids`, `Trail`, `Precipitation`, `compileClips`, `analyzeClip`, `spatialize`, `audio.loop`, `renderSound`.\n   - Plain-JS 240 Hz four-wheel sim (Appendix A), `RaceSim`, replay `Recording` at 30 Hz and compressed at 10 Hz, ghost traces.\n   - AI Hub session protocol 1, ghost mode, client 1.4.0, own card, harness conformance.\n   - Web Audio engine synth, gibberish formant voices, Song data.\n   - esbuild single-file build with unmodified `session.js` first in `<head>`.\n   - Playwright (Node) on software WebGPU with Chromium flags; pad stub; test runner with hash cache.\n   - Scratch-free dev folder; full test run exceeds 10 minutes, so run it with `run_in_background`.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - Root `work/`: `PLAN.md`, `STAGE.md`, `TASK.md`, `PROGRESS.md` (progress and issue log), `exit-interview.json`, `GranTumbleweed-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171904.html` (5.6 MB).\n   - Dev folder `GranTumbleweed-GranTurismo3ASpec-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-171904/` (`package.json`, `.gitignore`, `node_modules`, `docs/adding-content.md`, `docs/plan-audit.md`).\n   - `src/`: the kit (`pixel-engine.js` byte-identical to a fresh download, `check.mjs`, docs; starter `game.js` removed), `index.html`, `main.js` (`boot()`, `makeApp()`, `createGame()`, URL flags).\n   - `src/game/`:\n     - `app.js`, `flows.js` (all run start, finish and return logic).\n     - `sim/{sim,race,tuning,career,licence,setups,replaydata}.js`.\n     - `data/{meta,cars,carphysics,tyres,parts,tracks,licences,medals,events,text,songs,about,about.gen}.js`.\n     - `input/{pad,actions,prompts}.js`.\n     - `ui/{ui,widgets,glyphs,racehud}.js`.\n     - `ui/screens/{common,title,map,settings,handbook,about,licence,events,dealer,tuning,service,garage,museum,theatre,arcade,pause,results,cinema}.js`.\n     - `scenes/{menu,race,replay}.js`.\n     - `render/{geo,blocktext,trackmesh,dressing,world,sky,sets,carkit,carview,camera,director,people,pitcrew,particles,skid}.js`.\n     - `models/{cars,clips,buildings,props,sheets}.js`.\n     - `audio/{mix,enginesynth,sfx,voice,music}.js`.\n     - `net/{hub,couch}.js`, `dev/devmode.js`, `attract/director.js`.\n   - `tools/`:\n     - `build.mjs` (embeds exit-interview.json and CREDITS into `about.gen.js`).\n     - `test.mjs` (suites: sim, data, career, audit, headless, play, browser, audio, hub; `--force`; cache in `.testcache.json`).\n     - `audit.mjs` (writes `docs/plan-audit.md`; evidence kinds t: test, c: code, f: file, d: data compared with the plan, i: not a requirement, g: grep).\n     - `medals.mjs`, `shots.py` (1080p review shots to `shots/`), `hub-metas.html`, `hub/`.\n     - `tests/{_lib,_drive,_browser,sim,data,career,audit,headless,play,browser,audio,hub}.mjs`.\n   - Commands:\n     - `node tools/test.mjs [suite...] [--force]`\n     - `PLAY_VERBOSE=1 node tools/tests/play.mjs`\n     - `node tools/audit.mjs`\n     - `node tools/build.mjs`\n     - `node tools/medals.mjs`\n     - `cd src && node check.mjs --sheets game/models/sheets.js` (then remove `src/check`)\n   - Key balance and rule values:\n     - AI pace: dawn [.85,.89]; fellow [.86,.89]; feather hand-set `grid`; society [.87,.9]; touring [.91,.94]; h200 [.9,.93]; gp [.94,.97].\n     - Clay `rivals: [['ryu',.91],['ryu',.95],['ryuRally',.81]]`.\n     - Banked limit in `carProfile`: `mu = tyreMu*0.57*surf` where `|bank|>0.15`, passed as `vCap`.\n     - AI pace scales with wear: `skill *= sqrt(0.72+0.28*sqrt(meanWear))`.\n     - Human pit entry: `c.u>1 && t > halfW + (laps>=10 ? 0.3 : 3) && d>=pit.in-4 && d<pit.box-40`; AI `needsPit` only at `laps>=10`.\n     - Lead-in: barrier opens 60 m before the entry line.\n     - Medals from `tools/medals.mjs` (for example C-1 gold 18.71, silver 19.8, bronze 21.44); B-1 now starts at s:0 with the box at s:150.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - `aiDrive` was given race entries instead of car objects: fixed with `rawCars`.\n   - Box axis: long buildings came out rotated: fixed with `yaw + π/2`.\n   - `hud.sprite` undefined row from a negative tumbleweed frame: fixed with `Math.abs`.\n   - Seat colours as CSS strings crashed `hud.rect` on the hub card: fixed with numeric `SEAT_HUD`.\n   - Menu stack stayed over a race: `ui.clear()` in RaceScene setup.\n   - Pause closed on the press that opened it: 0.02 s lock in `ui.push`; the bot's `press` waits out the lock.\n   - Attract started right after a race: idle reset on Title and Map enter.\n   - Back did nothing on Pause and HubMenu: added `onBack`.\n   - Options in a hub round opened the pausing menu: `!app.session` guard; test added.\n   - Map click failed because the camera glided: camera holds still for the mouse, and clicks use the ray pick.\n   - Accidental pit entries, AI pitting in short races, unbeatable Red Clay and Featherweight fields, and Saltline overdriving (wall scraping and destroyed tyres): fixed as in the values above.\n   - Bot brain sampled too coarsely: now evaluated per fixed step.\n   - Clip feet went through the floor: foot IK tracks plus pelvis tuning.\n   - Screenshot fixes: tunnel walls culled, neon mirrored, HUD scanline panels illegible, rear tyres hidden, results buttons under the subtitle, hub card overflow in narrow frames (HUD auto-compact below 300 wide, card reflow), comb acne on set walls (only floors receive shadows).\n   - My own patch mistakes: inline `//` in Python patches commented out `installFlows` and broke `dressing.js`; stray `cat` hung a command and I stopped it with TaskStop; `testSetup` originally built a dummy RaceSim.\n   - Test mistakes: audio centroid DFT, career expectation, `tabs wrap`, hub frame filter matching the harness URL, the `.wav` grep matching `.wave`, stale-frame item counts.\n   - `check.mjs --sheets` refused a module outside `src/`: moved it to `src/game/models/sheets.js`.\n   - Hook feedback: earlier final reports were rejected as overstated or unverified. This led to three line-by-line audits (17 misses fixed) and `tools/audit.mjs`.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   All suites pass. The whole career finishes by bot with no Dev tools (about 193 s; about 27–33 in-game days depending on seed). Hub harness passes for 2 players, 4 players with latency, and host leaving, plus session menu, ghost follow, and music at 10%. The audit compares the plan's car, tyre, part, event, song, speaker, slider, clip and text data with game data. Evidence weakness to state honestly: some audit lines only confirm code is present, not behaviour. Remaining stated differences:\n   - AI pace lowered (the plan invites this).\n   - Banked limit (the plan suggests `vMax`).\n   - Pit lane needs a deliberate move in races under 10 laps; AI pits only at 10+.\n   - Body leans outward.\n   - B-3, A-2 and A-3 have short run-ins around C7–C10.\n   - Cone check is in `sim/licence.js`; voice function is `speak` via `mix.say`.\n   Limits (hardware, \"never required\"): no human drive, sound measured only, no real TV/phone/tablet, WebKit has no WebGPU here (only confirmed the message), balance tuned against a bot, rear-car shots at 960×540, and some tracks seen only in contact sheets.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - `/goal` command with args: \"Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.\"\n   - Stop hook feedback (harness, repeated across turns):\n     - (1) Insufficient evidence: contents of STAGE, PLAN and TASK not shown; hand-in list unchecked.\n     - (2) PLAN \"can be done here\" scope not verifiably complete, and the earlier report overstated.\n     - (3) PLAN and TASK never displayed in full; completeness asserted; two audits found gaps.\n     - (4, latest) \"The stopping condition is not shown as satisfied. The transcript never displays the full contents of PLAN.md or TASK.md; the audit that asserts every PLAN.md line is covered is the agent's own tool output, and the agent's final message lists known unmet items (no human play-test, sound never heard, WebKit WebGPU not run, a line-by-line audit that had already found 17 misses). The hand-in items are shown present, but the 'everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done' part is asserted rather than verified against the document text.\"\n   - Background-task notifications are system events, not user input.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Satisfy the Stop hook: show verification of PLAN.md and TASK.md against document text, and leave the final chat reply carrying the required sections. No new game work is known to be outstanding.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   The final build is verified: `tests: 1652 passed, 0 failed`, `audit: 488 lines, 0 failing, 55 not requirements on the game`, all hand-in checks PASS, HTML current, 0 leftover markers, 0 stray processes, scratch files removed, and PROGRESS.md item 12 marked done. The last reply gave the report below. Required content to repeat in any final reply:\n   - **Source Information:**\n     - Date: 2026-11-05 17:19:04\n     - Source: Gran Turismo 3: A-Spec\n     - Plan: PLAN.md\n     - Genre: racing simulation with a career, licences, tuning and car collecting\n     - Perspective: third-person chase camera (near and far), a bonnet view, TV cameras in replays; a three-quarter diorama camera in menus\n     - Tech: Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (riftlight kit), three.js r186 and Rapier 0.20 bundled in it, plain JavaScript modules, esbuild 0.28.2, AI Hub session client 1.4.0; Playwright 1.63.0 for tests only\n     - Art Direction: the engine's native 480×270 pixel art: low-poly toon shapes in the 16-colour Sweetie 16 palette with pixel outlines; 14 cars, 6 circuits, buildings and sets built from data and code\n     - Animation: the engine's rig and clips, plus 10 new clips (PitJack, PitWheelGun, PitFuel, MarshalFlag, ExaminerClipboard, DealerPresent, MechanicWipe, PodiumCheer, PodiumBow, CrowdCheer); procedural body roll, pit stops, dyno, car wash, Timeline ceremonies\n     - Audio: all in code, no audio files. A per-car engine synth on Web Audio, tyres, wind, rain, kerbs; 8 original songs and a fanfare, music at 10% by default; Animalese-style gibberish voices for 4 speakers with subtitles\n     - Online: AI Hub session protocol 1 in ghost mode (1–4 players, seat-coloured ghost cars, through the hub only), and Couch Duel: two players, split screen, one TV, no network\n     - Testing: headless and scripted tests allowed; one command printing failures and a count. Ran `node tools/test.mjs`: sim, data, career, audit (evidence for every line of the plan), headless, play (whole career by bot), browser (Chromium; WebKit only to confirm the no-WebGPU message), audio and hub (harness 2 players, 4 players with latency, host leaving, plus the session menu). Screenshots at 1920×1080 for what scripts can't judge. No subagents\n     - Research: no web research. Read the plan, the kit's GUIDE, ASSETS and parts of API, and the hub README\n     - Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n     - Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n     - Usage: added on import\n   - **Testing Feedback:**\n     - Issues: \"46 by my count: 13 from the scripted suites and the career bot, 3 from page errors in the browser, 13 from looking at screenshots, and 17 from three line-by-line reads of the plan that the tests had missed. No human has driven it, so handling feel is untested beyond scripted drivers: that is a real gap, not a pass.\"\n     - Critical: \"8: AI crash at the first race, menus left over the race, pause closing on the press that opened it, a crash on the hub card, boot broken by one of my own patches, the Red Clay and Featherweight fields that blocked the career, tyres destroyed on the Saltline banking in the Grand Prix, and Options in a hub round opening the pausing menu, which the harness did not catch.\"\n     - Most helpful: \"The whole-career bot. It played real screens through to the ending, and its log exposed accidental pit entries, unbeatable fields and the banking problem, none of which a unit test would have shown. Close behind it: tools/audit.mjs, which forced evidence for every line of the plan and found what passing tests had hidden.\"\n     - Least helpful: \"The audio centroid check was the least helpful: its one failure was a mistake in the test's own frequency analysis, and the engine synth was right from the start.\"\n   - **Engine Feedback:**\n     - Liked: \"Engine.headless: the same game code, in Node, without a GPU. It let a bot play the whole career in about three minutes, and the HUD, particles and tweens all just worked there.\"\n     - Stuck: \"Yes. I assumed a box's width runs along its heading, so long buildings came out sideways until I saw a screenshot. I passed the wrong arguments to analyzeClip by guessing its signature. Screen transitions never resolve while the engine is in manual step mode, which hung one of my scripts.\"\n     - Built: \"The driving: the car model came with the plan, and on it I built the race rules, career, replay director, track mesh and dressing, and the engine synth. For the engine's own part, the menu system with focus, prompts and glyphs was the largest thing I had to build.\"\n     - Hardest: \"Getting a large world built from one GeoBatch to look right with shadows: acne on walls at grazing angles, and the choice of which surfaces may receive them. Also the HUD's colour argument: a CSS string fails only at paint time, outside the game's hooks, so engine.errors stays empty.\"\n     - Changed: \"none\".\n     - One change: \"Have hud.rect and hud.sprite reject bad input (an unknown colour name, an undefined sprite row) at the call, with a message, instead of throwing later in the paint step where the stack says nothing about the game.\"\n   - **Prompt Feedback:**\n     - Liked: \"The plan's section on engine facts and its tested Appendix A. Knowing what was already proven let the risk go where it belonged, and the sim matched every regression number on the first run.\"\n     - Confusing: \"The brief's title rule against the plan's rule on the source's name took a moment to reconcile. Also 'Online' as a bare URL, though the plan explains it. And the plan's roll sign in recipe C3 reads as if the body leans into a corner; I used the outward lean, which is what the sim's load transfer implies.\"\n     - Hardest: \"Judging feel with no human and no real pad. Balance can be measured with a bot; whether the car is nice to steer cannot. The next hardest was making a full career finishable with sensible money without cheating.\"\n     - Change: \"I would ask for a short human play-test note to be handed on, since the brief makes it impossible here, and I would state outright that a bot's skill 1.0 is stronger than most people, because the plan's AI pace ranges were too high for it even so.\"\n   - aiHub block in the file: name GRAN TUMBLEWEED; tagline The Peninsula Driving Catalogue; type Game; genre Racing; source Gran Turismo 3: A-Spec; series Gran Turismo; players 1–4; controls \"Stick or WASD to steer, R2 or W to go, L2 or S to brake\"; tags 3d, pixel art, vehicles, retro, third-person, versus.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   The Stop hook's latest complaint is: \"the 'everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done' part is asserted rather than verified against the document text.\" The next step, once tools are available, is to print PLAN.md sections 1–18 and TASK.md verbatim beside `docs/plan-audit.md` in tool output, so the check is visible against the document text. Where audit rows rest only on \"code present\", I'd upgrade the ones that can be tested behaviourally on this computer, fixing any real gap that turns up and rerunning `node tools/test.mjs` (use `run_in_background`, since a full run exceeds 10 minutes). Then I'd give the final report with the sections above, stating plainly that the hardware limits are \"never required\" under TASK.md rather than open items, and without overclaiming.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-